flex NEWS

* Noteworthy changes in release  ?.? (????-??-??) [?]

** build

*** Building flex no longer rewrites the .po translation files.

*** A race condition that could build flex twice at the same time during
    a parallel make has been fixed.

*** Source files now carry SPDX license identifiers.

    Thanks to Tuukka Pasanen.

*** The replacement malloc in lib/ now has a full prototype.

    Thanks to Richard Barnes.

*** Automated builds now run on GitHub Actions instead of travis-ci, and
    nightly distribution tarballs are built there.

    Thanks to Joseph Langley.

*** The configure script accepts the space that AC_PROG_LEX puts before
    the parentheses when checking for yymore and yyreject. It also no
    longer uses obsolete Autoconf macros, and the macro that finds the C
    compiler for the build machine has been updated to fix problems with
    executable file names when cross compiling.

    Thanks to Bill Currie and Kang-Che Sung.

*** The man page can now be built when cross compiling, including on
    MS Windows. (#560)

*** Various edge cases, mostly involving out-of-tree builds have been
    accounted for in the autotools build system.

    Thanks to Michael Haubenwallner and Kang-Che Sung.

*** The build now has flex build its own scanner a second time and fails
    if the result differs from the first. This check runs only for
    native builds.

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** The GitHub Actions workflows now use pinned versions of the actions,
    have read-only permissions, and are kept up to date by dependabot.

    Thanks to Pedro Kaj Kjellerup Nacht.

*** The minimum versions of the Autotools needed to build flex are now
    correct and documented, and autogen.sh is more reliable in finding
    libtoolize.

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** Flex no longer searches PATH for m4 itself and leaves that to the
    system. This avoids a failure on 32-bit systems that read file
    systems with 64-bit inode numbers. (#413)

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** Flex no longer declares reallocarray() itself.

    Thanks to Lukasz Baj.

*** Flex now includes its own <config.h> header before including system
    headers so that any system specific features detected by configure
    are taken into account during compilation of flex itself.

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** The flex build system now includes Makefile targets at the top level
    to allow just building and installing libfl.

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** The flex distribution now includes a file, src/libfl.pc, to allow
    using pkgconfig to find out what flags to use when building against
    libfl from flex.

    Thanks to Tyler Slabinski and Kang-Che Sung.

*** The skeleton files are now generated correctly on systems whose
    files use CRLF line endings.

    Thanks to Jannick.

*** A crash during building on NetBSD has been fixed.

    Thanks to Thomas Klausner.

** documentation

*** Several examples in the manual have been corrected or modernized:
    character classes, case-insensitive ranges, the patterns for C and
    C++ comments, the Bison snippets, and a call to yylex_init_extra().

    Thanks to Joseph Langley, Martin Joerg, zmajeed, Akim Demaille and
    Nikolaos Chatzikonstantinou.

*** Typos in the manual and the README have been fixed.

    Thanks to Houtan Bastani, Xiao Hanyu, winter.loo, musvaage and
    6NoFantasy9.

*** INSTALL is now INSTALL.md and has updated instructions. There is a
    new CONTRIBUTING.md, and the README now describes how to use the
    mailing list and where to find releases.

    Thanks to Joseph Langley and Simon Sobisch.

*** New bg, ka, pt, uk translations from the Translation Project.

*** Updated ca, da, de, eo, es, fi, fr, ga, hr, ko, nl, pl, pt_BR, ro,
    ru, sr, sv, tr, vi, zh_CN, zh_TW translations from the Translation
    Project.

*** The --yymore option is now documented.

    Thanks to ggujjula.

*** The manual now describes how to retarget flex to other languages and
    lists the deprecated interfaces and their replacements.

    Thanks to Eric S. Raymond and Daniel Brooks.

** scanner

*** The --skel (-S) option again makes flex read the skeleton file it
    names. Flex had been accepting the option but using its built-in
    skeleton anyway.

*** Scanners that use REJECT no longer write past the end of their
    state buffer when a buffer larger than the default is installed
    with yypush_buffer_state() and a long token is then scanned. (#752)

    Thanks to anshal ossaimi.

*** C++ scanners that use REJECT now allocate their state buffer with
    yyalloc() and release it with yyfree(), as C scanners do. Before,
    switching to an input buffer larger than the default could pass
    memory obtained with new[] to yyrealloc(), and a scanner that
    defines its own yyalloc(), yyrealloc() and yyfree() received a
    pointer its yyalloc() had never returned. (#751)

    Thanks to anshal ossaimi.

*** Flex no longer writes one byte past the end of a buffer when the
    name of the output file given with -o is very long and contains
    enough backslashes or double quotes. (#749)

    Thanks to ReisterJ.

*** The messages Flex prints are easier to translate, and more of them
    are offered to the Translation Project. The --help text is split
    into several messages. Words that must not be translated, such as
    option and keyword names, are no longer part of the messages. The
    statistics printed by -v and the dumps printed in debug mode are
    no longer marked for translation, so they appear in English. The
    messages in five source files that were never offered for
    translation now are, and the word "warning" at the start of each
    warning is translated. Seven short error messages, such as
    "premature EOF" and "bad character class", now say what is wrong.

*** The --help text showed the -T option as --env.trace, and the message
    about using -l with --reentrant called that option --ctrl.reentrant.
    Both now show the right option names.

*** Scanners generated with --tables-file or --tables-verify now check
    the tables file they read at run time. A damaged or hostile file
    could make such a scanner read outside its memory, write outside it,
    or ask for a huge amount of it. Now yytables_fload() returns -1 for
    such a file. A scanner that is already built is not changed, so it
    must be generated again with this version of Flex. (#730)

    Thanks to MarkLee131.

*** With %array, a switch to another input buffer now ends the text
    saved by yymore(), as it does with %pointer. Before, calling yyless()
    with a count shorter than that text after the switch could move the
    scanner to before the start of the new buffer.

*** With %array, calling yyless() with a count shorter than the text
    saved by yymore() now returns the rest of that text to the input, as
    it does with %pointer. Before, that text was dropped and never
    scanned again. With yylineno, the newlines in it are counted once.

*** With %option yylineno, a newline matched by the default rule is now
    counted in yylineno. Before, yylineno did not change when the
    default rule matched a newline. (#397)

    Thanks to ggujjula; reported by Horst H. von Brand.

*** With %array, calling yyless() after yymore() could write past the
    end of the input buffer. This has been fixed.

    Thanks to anshal ossaimi.

*** A heap buffer overflow that could happen when flex_main() was called
    more than once in the same process has been fixed.

    Thanks to David Pokora of Trail of Bits, in collaboration with
    Anthropic.

*** Flex no longer puts #line directives from %top blocks into the
    output when --noline or %option noline is given. (#463, #268)

    Thanks to Javier Maestro.

*** A bug in how yylex_destroy() frees the buffer stack has been fixed.

    Thanks to Wiktor Garbacz.

*** Refilling the input buffer is a little faster.

    Thanks to John Lindal.

*** When the input buffer is grown, the new size now always includes
    room for the final two end-of-buffer characters.

    Thanks to David Lamparter.

*** Generated scanners now compile without warnings in more situations,
    for example with -Wextra-semi-stmt (#416), -Wundef,
    -Wnull-dereference (#377), when yyunput is disabled, and when
    comparing sizes.

    Thanks to Sean McBride, Alex Richardson, Kang-Che Sung and Bill
    Currie.

*** The error message for a file that cannot be opened now includes the
    system's reason.

    Thanks to Ryan McCullagh.

*** Some memory leaks have been fixed.

    Thanks to viktor.shepel and Kang-Che Sung.

*** Scanners generated with the --full option no longer allocate table
    space that is never used. (#628)

    Thanks to Joseph Langley.

*** Line numbers in #line directives were off by one after an indented
    code block in the rules section. This has been fixed.

    Thanks to Bill Currie.

*** A leftover reference to the old location of the line directive
    template in the output filter was corrected.

    Thanks to Burke Libbey.

*** A crash while starting m4 on Apple M1 has been fixed. (#539)

    Thanks to Yifeng Li.

*** Scanners now fail with an error, rather than misbehaving, when
    yy_get_next_buffer() cannot find a buffer. (#525)

    Thanks to Joseph Langley.

*** The generated header flexint_shared.h can now be included twice
    without errors (#329), and it uses <cstdint> when compiled as C++11
    or later. (#517)

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** New options: --noinput and --noyyinput, --noyyget_debug and
    --noyyset_debug. The existing --nounput can now also be written
    --noyyunput.

    Thanks to ggujjula and Eric S. Raymond.

*** Scanners that call yy_switch_to_buffer() with REJECT now always have
    a large enough state buffer.

    Thanks to Joseph Langley.

*** There are new function style interfaces to replace those that relied
    on C macros: yybegin(), yyecho(), yyreject(), yyinput(), yyunput(),
    yystart(), yyatbol(), yysetbol() and yypanic(). Macros that you used
    to define yourself are replaced by options: yydecl, yylmax,
    noyyread, extra-type, user-init, pre-action, post-action and
    bufsize. The old interfaces still work with the default back end but
    are deprecated, as is YY_FLEX_SUBMINOR_VERSION. The manual lists the
    replacements.

    Thanks to Eric S. Raymond.

*** Flex can now generate scanners in other languages. The new option
    -e, --emit (also %option emit) selects a back end. The default back
    end generates C or C++ as before. A new C99 back end generates only
    reentrant scanners and drops the legacy interfaces. The new %option
    rewrite controls whether calls to the function style interfaces
    below are rewritten for back ends that cannot rely on the C
    preprocessor.

    Thanks to Eric S. Raymond.

*** Generated scanners no longer need a separate table for NUL
    transitions.

    Thanks to Nick Downing.

*** The indentation in the skeleton has been corrected.

    Thanks to Joerg Sonnenberger.

*** YYTABLES_NAME is no longer defined unless tables are requested.

    Thanks to Jannick.

*** yy_top_state() now returns the current start condition when the
    start condition stack is empty, including in reentrant scanners.

    Thanks to Jannick.

*** An option argument can now be "--", as in "flex --outfile -- file".
    (#285)

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** Memory for filters is now allocated with calloc.

    Thanks to Michael W. Bombardieri.

*** A long standing bug that effected expressions of the form c{i,j}
    where 'c' is a character and {i,j} describes the number of times to
    match against 'c' when case sensitivity was turned on has been
    fixed.

    Thanks to NieDzejkob.

*** New option: --backup-file allows setting the name of the file
    written containing backing up information. Useful if you need
    backing up information from multiple scanners in the same directory.

    Thanks to EricSharkey.

*** flex emits correct line number directives when line numbers refer to
    the file containing the line number directives.

    Thanks to Jeff Smith.

*** The options {no,}yy{get,set}_column are now supported.

** test

*** The test suite now works in VPATH builds, and the test programs it
    generates are ignored by git.

    Thanks to Joseph Langley.

*** The test suite no longer fails to build when many jobs run in
    parallel and finish in an unlucky order, because the parser objects
    now depend on the scanner headers they include.

    Thanks to Ross Burton.

*** The shell scripts in the repository have been cleaned up using
    shellcheck.

    Thanks to Daniel Brooks.

*** Many tests are now generated from *.rules files, so that each one
    runs against every back end. When running "make check", you can set
    TESTOPTS to pass options to flex.

    Thanks to Eric S. Raymond.

*** Generating the various tableoptions make rules is now more portable.

    Thanks to Kang-Che Sung.

*** Tests of the options -C*f and -C*F will now run correctly on
    filesystems that are not case sensitive.

    Thanks to Hans-Bernhard Broeker.

* Noteworthy changes in release 2.6.4 (2017-05-06) [stable]

** build

*** The indent target now knows about flex's new (as of 2.6.0)
    layout. The indent rules it would apply are not correct and do
    need to be fixed.

*** The files included in the flex distribution are now built by the
    version of flex that is included in the distribution.

*** The configure script has a better idea of which headers are
    required to build flex. It will also error when missing functions
    are detected.

*** We have lowered the versions of automake and gettext that
    configure.ac lists as required for building flex. In autogen.sh,
    we now check for how to call libtoolize and use what we find in
    the rest of the script.

*** Since files in lib/ are picked up as needed by src/, we no longer
    generate a Makefile for that directory.

*** Flex can be cross compiled.

** documentation

*** Some typos were removed from the manual.

** scanner

*** Some minor performance enhancements.

*** We honor user defined yy_* macros again. We are also more careful
    to not leak macro definitions into header files.

*** A number of portability fixes were introduced so building flex is
    more reliable on more platforms. Additionally, outdated function
    calls were removed.

*** When building the flex executable itself, %# comments from
    flex.skl are removed when generating the C source code array. This
    reduces the size of flex.

** test suite

*** All scripts in the test suite are now run by $(SHELL) and the
    needed portability fixes have been included.

*** Test suite dependencies are handled much better. This only matters
    if you are actively developing flex or its test suite.

*** Tests that depend on platform dependent features now properly skip
    when those platforms are not present.

*** When running "make check", you can now pas V=0 to silence more of
    the build. This is useful when you're less concerned about the
    details of building and linking the test programs themselves.

* Noteworthy changes in release 2.6.3 (2016-12-30) [stable]

** scanner

*** several bug fixes resolved problems introduced in recent flex
    versions regarding processing of comments, literals and various
    quoting scenarios.

*** If the path to m4 was sufficiently long, a buffer overflow could
    occur. This has been resolved. The fix also removes dependence on
    the constant PATH_MAX.

** build

*** A new configure option --disable-bootstrap changes the behavior of
    the build system when building flex. The default
    "--enable-bootstrap" behavior is to build flex, then to use that
    flex to build flex again. With --disable-bootstrap, the scanner is
    simply built by sedding the scanner source. This is friendlier to
    cross compilation.

*** The compatibility functions in lib/ are no longer built as a
    library. Instead, they are built as $(LIBOBJ) objects. This is
    simpler and friendlier to cross compilation.

*** It is now possible to build flex without building the accompanying
    libfl. This is friendlier to cross compilation. See the
    --disable-libfl option to configure. Resolves #99.

*** the PIC version of libfl was not correctly built. It is no longer
    included in the build/installation targets of flex since it was
    unused.

*** the distributed man page is only rebuilt when the relevant source
    files change or when the binary doesn't exist. In particular, this
    is friendlier to cross compilation. Resolves #108

** test

*** the shell scripts in the test suite are more portable across different shell implementations.

* version 2.6.2 released 2016-10-24

** flex internals

*** a segfault involving yyrestart(NULL) has been fixed

*** flex should now handle quoting when mixed with m4 processing correctly

*** flex handles `[[' and `]]' correctly

*** flex no longer generates non-ANSI code

*** more compilation warnings were squashed in generated scanners

*** prevented a buffer overflow that could occur when input buffers were the exact wrong size

** test suite

*** input filenames on MSWindows are now calculated correctly

*** general code cleanups in a number of tests now make the test suite compile much more cleanly

** build system

*** the xz archive has been replaced with an lzip archive

*** a new option to configure --enable-warnings to encapsulate passing
    of warning-related flags which is useful in testing flex

*** make indent now works for out of source builds

*** Portability warnings when generating Makefile.in files are now suppressed; they were just noise and the use of GNU extensions in Makefile.{am,in,} was intentional and well known.

** bugs

*** resolved gh#67

** new sv translation from the translation project

* version 2.6.1 released 2016-03-01

** flex resources

*** The flex project is now hosted at github. Consider this a "period of transition". In particular, you should start at https://github.com/westes/flex for the flex codebase, issue tracking and pull requests.

*** New releases of flex are to be found at https://github.com/westes/flex/releases.

** flex internals

*** Flex now uses more modern and more standard names for variable types. There's more work to be done on that front yet, though.

*** A number of compiler warnings have been remedied.

*** Line directives should now  work as expected and be absent when that is expected.

** test suite

*** When running the test suite, c++ files are compiled with the c++ header inside the flex distribution, rather than relying on the build system's flex header , which might not be installed yet or which might be out of date with respect to what flex tests expect.

*** Some portability fixes in the test suite such as opening files for reading in binary mode

** Building flex

*** The file src/scan.c asdistributed  with flex source is now built with the current version of flex. Occasionally this had to be done manually to pick up new flex features. It's now just a part of flex's build system.

*** The pdf version of the manual is no longer distributed with flex, although if you have the texinfo package installed, you can still build it.

*** lots of general build system cleanup

*** the build system tries a bit harder to find libtoolize and texi2dvi.

*** When help2man and texi2dvi are missing, the error messages are now much more helpful.

** bug fixes

*** resolved github issues #53, #54, #55, #61.

*** Resolved sf bugs #128, #129, #155, #160, #184, #187, #195.

* version 2.6.0 released 2015-11-17

** User Visible Changes

*** C++ scanners now use references instead of pointers. See the manual for details.

*** A number of compiler warnings were addressed, so flex generated scanners should be quieter under compiler warning scenarios.

*** Allow error reporting routines to accept varying number of arguments

*** Removed deprecated 'register' storage class specifier

*** Changed output formats from octal to hexadecimal

*** check limits before using array index cclp; resolves sf-166

*** Suppress clang warning about empty @param paragraph; resolves sf#158

*** Fixed malloc/realloc replacement, resolves sf bug#151.

*** Adjusted buffer sizes on ia64.

*** various documentation and code clean up fixes: resolves sf bugs #167, #168, among other patches.

** Flex Internals

*** flex is now organized into subdirectories. This keeps the tree neater at the top level and puts like things near each other and unlike things away from each other.

*** The test suite has been reorganized and is now run with the parallel test suite harness from automake.

*** Cleaned up the automake parts of the build system to better reflect what automake does on its own. Also added a call to libtoolize in autogen.sh because autoreconf gets confused without a prior run of libtoolize.

*** po/Makefile now includes a rule to fetch the latest translations from the translation project. "make -f po/Makefile getpo" from the top level of the flex tree will fetch the files.

*** New da translation from the translation project

